<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<refentry xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2"
          xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
          xml:id="ndm_decomposecollisions">

  <info>
    <productname>neurosuite-3</productname>
    <authorgroup>
      <author><personname><firstname>neurosuite-3</firstname>
        <surname>contributors</surname></personname></author>
    </authorgroup>
    <copyright><year>2025</year><holder>neurosuite-3 contributors</holder></copyright>
    <legalnotice><para>GPL-3.0-or-later</para></legalnotice>
  </info>

  <refmeta>
    <refentrytitle>ndm_decomposecollisions</refentrytitle>
    <manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
  </refmeta>

  <refnamediv>
    <refname>ndm_decomposecollisions</refname>
    <refpurpose>
      Detect and decompose spike collision waveforms into single-unit components
    </refpurpose>
  </refnamediv>

  <refsynopsisdiv>
    <cmdsynopsis>
      <command>ndm_decomposecollisions</command>
      <arg choice="opt"><replaceable>session.yaml</replaceable></arg>
      <arg choice="opt"><option>--method</option> <replaceable>M</replaceable></arg>
    </cmdsynopsis>
  </refsynopsisdiv>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Description</title>
    <para>
      <command>ndm_decomposecollisions</command> identifies spike waveforms
      that are composed of two overlapping action potentials ("collisions")
      from two different units and decomposes each into its two
      single-unit components.
      It operates post-curation: the curated
      <filename>.clu.<replaceable>method</replaceable>.N</filename> files
      provide the unit labels from which mean waveform templates are derived.
      The <filename>.clu</filename>, <filename>.res</filename> and
      <filename>.spk</filename> files are never modified.
    </para>
    <para>
      When two neurons fire within approximately one spike-width of each other
      (roughly 0.5–1 ms at typical sampling rates), their extracellular
      waveforms sum linearly at each electrode site.  The composite waveform
      occupies an intermediate region of PCA feature space and is commonly
      misassigned — either to the dominant parent cluster or to the noise
      cluster — during automated sorting.
    </para>
    <para>
      All template matching is restricted to spikes within a single
      spikeDetection group (same tetrode or polytrode shank).  Cross-shank
      collisions are not attempted.
    </para>
    <para>
      The script reads its parameters and calls
      <command>process_decomposecollisions</command>, a C++ program (OpenMP
      on the CPU; the CUDA build is used when it was compiled, otherwise
      <command>process_decomposecollisions_cpu</command>).  The full
      reference is <filename>doc/ndmanager-plugins/commands/ndm_decomposecollisions.md</filename>.
    </para>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Algorithm</title>

    <refsect2>
      <title>Stage 1 — Template building</title>
      <para>
        For each curated unit (clusters 0 and 1 are left out when
        <parameter>excludeNoise</parameter> is true) with at least
        <parameter>minSpikesTemplate</parameter> spikes, the mean waveform
        template
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>T_k</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        is computed, together with the 1st and 99th percentiles of the unit's
        amplitude distribution.  A group with fewer than two templates is
        skipped.
      </para>
    </refsect2>

    <refsect2>
      <title>Stage 2 — Screening</title>
      <para>
        The noise RMS is estimated over all waveform samples as
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>median(|x|) / 0.6745</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        (Quiroga 2004).  Spikes whose peak-to-peak amplitude is below
        <parameter>minSnrRms</parameter> × RMS are not screened.  For the
        others, the Pearson r between the spike and its own cluster's
        template (no shift; limited to
        ±<parameter>corrWindow</parameter> samples around the peak sample
        when <parameter>corrWindow</parameter> &gt; 0) is computed.  A spike
        with r below <parameter>corrThreshold</parameter> is a collision
        candidate.  Spikes of a cluster without a template are recorded
        without a decomposition.
      </para>
    </refsect2>

    <refsect2>
      <title>Stage 3 — Two-component fit</title>
      <para>
        <emphasis role="bold">Pass 1</emphasis>: component 1 is the spike's
        own cluster at zero shift, with a least-squares amplitude
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>a₁ = ⟨W, T_k⟩ / ‖T_k‖²</mathphrase></inlineequation>,
        giving the residual
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>R₁ = W − a₁·T_k</mathphrase></inlineequation>.
      </para>
      <para>
        <emphasis role="bold">Pass 2</emphasis>: every template of a
        <emphasis>different</emphasis> unit is fitted to
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>R₁</mathphrase></inlineequation> over
        shifts τ ∈ [−<parameter>maxShiftSamp</parameter>,
        +<parameter>maxShiftSamp</parameter>] (only the overlapping samples
        are used); the one with the smallest residual and a positive
        amplitude is component 2.  Same-unit pairs (bursts) are not
        considered.
      </para>
      <para>
        Amplitudes are clamped to the unit's 1st–99th percentile range
        widened by half its width on each side.  Both components get a
        parabolic sub-sample shift estimate.  A decomposition is
        <emphasis>accepted</emphasis> when
        <inlineequation><mathphrase>‖R₂‖ / ‖W‖ &lt; residualThreshold</mathphrase></inlineequation>
        and a second unit was found.  Every candidate, accepted or not, is
        written to the <filename>.col</filename> file.
      </para>
    </refsect2>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Prerequisites</title>
    <itemizedlist>
      <listitem><para>
        Spike sorting must be complete and manual curation in
        <application>Klusters</application> must be finished.
        <filename>.clu.<replaceable>method</replaceable>.N</filename>,
        <filename>.res.N</filename> and <filename>.spk.N</filename> files
        must exist.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        Each spikeDetection group must contain at least two units with at
        least <parameter>minSpikesTemplate</parameter> spikes each.  Groups
        with fewer are skipped.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        <command>process_decomposecollisions</command> must be installed
        (built with the ndmanager-plugins; CUDA optional).  A YAML session
        file is required; there is no XML fallback.
      </para></listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Parameters</title>
    <para>
      Read from the <literal>ndm_decomposecollisions</literal> block of the
      session's <literal>programs</literal> section.
    </para>
    <variablelist>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>maxShiftSamp</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Largest shift (± samples) tried for component 2.  At 32 kHz the
          default covers ±0.31 ms.  Runtime grows roughly as
          O(maxShiftSamp × n_templates).
          (Default: 10)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>corrThreshold</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Correlation with the own-cluster template below which a spike is a
          collision candidate.  Raise toward 0.92 to flag only the most
          obvious collisions; lower toward 0.75 to widen the candidate pool.
          (Default: 0.85)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>residualThreshold</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Largest accepted
          <inlineequation><mathphrase>‖R₂‖ / ‖W‖</mathphrase></inlineequation>.
          0.25 means the two templates must explain at least 75 % of the
          waveform norm.
          (Default: 0.25)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>minSnrRms</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Minimum peak-to-peak amplitude, in units of the noise RMS, for a
          spike to be screened.
          (Default: 4.0)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>minSpikesTemplate</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Minimum spikes for a unit to contribute a template.
          (Default: 30)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>corrWindow</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Half-width (samples) around the peak sample used for the screening
          correlation; 0 uses the whole waveform.
          (Default: 0)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>excludeNoise</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          When <literal>true</literal>, clusters 0 (noise) and 1 (MUA) are
          left out of the templates.
          (Default: true)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>overwrite</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          When <literal>false</literal>, groups whose
          <filename>.col</filename> file already exists are skipped.
          (Default: false)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>nWorkers</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          CPU threads; 0 uses all cores.
          (Default: 0)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>useGpu</parameter></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Use the CUDA path when the program was built with CUDA.
          (Default: false)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><parameter>method</parameter>, <option>--method</option> <replaceable>M</replaceable></term>
        <listitem><para>
          File variant: <literal>standard</literal>, <literal>sdiff</literal>,
          <literal>stderiv</literal>, <literal>stderiv_S</literal><replaceable>n</replaceable>
          or <literal>stderiv_C</literal><replaceable>n</replaceable>.
          The command-line option overrides the parameter.
          (Default: standard)
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>

    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Output File Format</title>
    <para>
      One <filename>SESSION.col.<replaceable>method</replaceable>.N</filename>
      file is written per spikeDetection group.  It is binary: a 32-byte
      header starting with the magic <literal>COL\x01</literal>, a parameter
      block, one row per template and one 60-byte record per candidate
      (spike index and time, own cluster and correlation, accepted flag,
      residual norm, and unit, integer shift, fractional shift and
      amplitude of each component).  The layout is in
      <filename>doc/ndmanager-plugins/formats/col.md</filename>.
    </para>
    <para>
      For groups with at least one accepted decomposition,
      <filename>SESSION.eap.N</filename> and <filename>SESSION.tcl.N</filename>
      are created or updated as well (see the command reference).
    </para>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Downstream Use</title>
    <para>
      The <filename>.col.N</filename> sidecar provides two downstream options
      without altering the original spike-sorting files:
    </para>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Exclusion</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spikes whose record has the accepted flag set
          are removed from firing-rate computations and cross-correlograms.
          This is the conservative approach and is always safe.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term>Reassignment</term>
        <listitem><para>
          Each accepted collision spike at time <emphasis>t</emphasis> is
          replaced by two events: one for unit k₁ at
          <emphasis>t + sh₁</emphasis> and one for unit k₂ at
          <emphasis>t + sh₂</emphasis> (add the fractional shift for
          sub-sample precision).  This
          recovers spikes that would otherwise be permanently lost from both
          units' spike trains.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
    <para>
      For cross-correlogram analysis, excluding accepted collisions recovers
      the true refractory dip between units and removes the spurious excess
      coincidences that confound functional connectivity inference.
    </para>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Limitations</title>
    <itemizedlist>
      <listitem><para>
        Three-component collisions (three neurons firing within a single
        waveform window) are not attempted.  They are rare (&lt; 0.1 % of
        spikes in typical recordings) and the two-pass pursuit will usually
        assign the pair with the highest combined energy, leaving a
        detectable residual.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        The algorithm assumes that within-collision amplitude variability
        follows the same distribution as single-unit amplitude variability.
        For units with very narrow amplitude distributions the amplitude
        clamp may be too tight to fit a collision well.
      </para></listitem>
      <listitem><para>
        Template drift over the course of a long recording degrades
        decomposition accuracy.  Run
        <citerefentry>
          <refentrytitle>ndm_estimatedrift</refentrytitle>
          <manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
        </citerefentry>
        first and inspect the drift output.  Sessions with drift &gt; 10 µm/h
        should be corrected or split into shorter segments before collision
        decomposition.
      </para></listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
  </refsect1>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <refsect1>
    <title>Files</title>
    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.yaml</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Session parameter file (mandatory input).  The
          <literal>spikeDetection.channelGroups</literal> section is read
          for per-group <literal>nSamples</literal>,
          <literal>peakSampleIndex</literal>, and channel lists.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.clu.<replaceable>method</replaceable>.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Curated cluster labels (mandatory input).  Read strictly under the
          selected method.
          Must reflect post-curation single-unit assignments.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.res.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spike timestamps in samples (mandatory input).  This and the
          <filename>.spk</filename> file are looked up under the method, then
          <literal>standard</literal>, then the untagged name, then any other
          method tag.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.spk.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Spike waveforms — int16 binary, row-major (spike × sample × site)
          (mandatory input).  Waveform data is required; the plugin exits with
          an error if no <filename>.spk.N</filename> files are found.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename>SESSION.col.<replaceable>method</replaceable>.N</filename></term>
        <listitem><para>
          Output collision sidecar file (binary, see above).  One per spikeDetection group.
          Contains global statistics, template metadata, and per-spike
          decomposition records.
        </para></listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

